The page sits in Grothendieck's 'gammes sur les radicaux', part of the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'. The setting is apparently a rank-one reductive group G (likely GL2), with its special and projective forms SG and PG. Attached to a pair of conjugacy or torus data (ρ, σ) are group schemes:
- R_{ρ,σ}, called 'radicaux';
- SR_{ρ,σ}, its special version;
- M_{ρ,σ} and M^+_{ρ,σ};
- T_{ρ,σ} and T^+_{ρ,σ}, which are torus-like;
- the 'connected' or 'neutral' versions of each, marked with a superscript ° (R°, SR°, M°, M^{+°}, T°, T^{+°}).

Diagram (117) is a cube refining cube (116) of the previous page. Its arrows are:
- arrows labelled G_m (SR→R, M^+→M, T^+→T, and the ° analogues), which are G_m-torsors or central extensions;
- arrows labelled SG (SR→M^+, SR°→M^{+°});
- arrows labelled PG (R→M);
- dashed composites labelled G (SR→M);
- vertical arrows labelled μ2×μ2 linking the ° layer to the non-° layer, i.e. finite étale μ2×μ2 (component-group) extensions.

The previous page states that the lateral faces are cartesian. On this page Grothendieck observes the following.
- The 'front-left' face is especially simple. Its middle terms are direct products of the extreme terms with SG: M^+_{ρ,σ} ≃ SR_{ρ,σ} × SG and M^{+°}_{ρ,σ} ≃ SR°_{ρ,σ} × SG.
- These extreme terms satisfy SR ≃ T^+ (≃ S'T_ρ × SN'_σ, partly illegible) and SR° ≃ T^{+°} ≃ ST_ρ × ST_σ. So the SG-torsors there are trivial.
- The parallel 'back-right' face is almost of the same kind. It becomes literally a product only after replacing the middle terms M, M° by pro-coverings (pro-revêtements): \tilde M ≃ R × SG and \tilde M° ≃ R° × SG (118), referring back to (103) and (107).

In modern terms, these are splittings of the torsors or extensions in a cube of algebraic groups after passing to a (pro-)covering. This is the familiar device of passing from PG-type quotients to their simply connected or isogeny covers so that quotient maps become trivial product projections.

The next page goes on to build a canonical homomorphism G_m → R° through G_m → T° ≃ T_ρ ×_{G_m} T_σ, λ ↦ (λ, λ), whose composite with the norm-type character is λ ↦ λ².
